+# smallpt — Rendering Lab
+
+Kevin Beason's open-source C++ path tracer compiled to WebAssembly, displayed in a responsive, static browser app. Open the app through your existing static web hosting. No backend, runtime package dependencies, or cross-origin isolation headers are required. Browsers require HTTP(S) for the module worker and Wasm fetch; opening `index.html` as a `file://` URL is unsupported.
+
+The page automatically renders the original Cornell box. **Randomize & render** changes the mirror and glass spheres' reflectance colors, generates a fresh random seed, and starts another render. A new render replaces any ongoing render. Choose samples and resolution, then select **Render scene** to apply them. **Reset** restores the original white spheres and default settings. The download control saves the current canvas as PNG.
+
+## Build
+
+The compiled `assets/smallpt.wasm` is checked in and ready to serve. Rebuilding requires Node.js, Clang with the wasm32 target, and LLVM lld:
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+npm test
+```
+
+The build uses `clang++-19` and `wasm-ld` by default. Set `CXX` and `WASM_LD` to other compiler/linker paths if needed:
+
+```sh
+CXX=clang++ WASM_LD=/path/to/wasm-ld npm run build
+```
+
+`node scripts/make-preview.mjs` optionally rebuilds the initial 640 × 480, 128-sample PNG with the same compiled renderer. This image is only a placeholder until the first live pixels arrive.
+
+## Implementation
+
+- `src/vendor/smallpt.cpp`: unmodified upstream source, downloaded from https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/.
+- `src/vendor/LICENSE.txt`: upstream MIT license, copyright Kevin Beason.
+- `src/browser.cpp`: progressive rendering adapter and RGBA framebuffer. Keeps original scene geometry, radiance calculation, reflection/refraction, Russian roulette, gamma correction, and 2 × 2 subpixel sampling.
+- `scripts/build-wasm.mjs`: combines the upstream rendering core with the adapter and a POSIX-compatible `erand48` implementation, replacing the original command-line entry point and PPM output. Compiles without libc or an Emscripten runtime. Only `sin`, `cos`, and `pow` are imported from JavaScript; `sqrt` uses the Wasm instruction.
+- `assets/render-worker.js`: loads Wasm in a module worker and transfers progressive RGBA snapshots to the page. Computation stays off the main thread. Rendering uses one worker and no OpenMP.
+- `assets/app.js`: controls, canvas presentation, worker lifecycle, errors, and PNG downloads.
+
+The renderer accumulates samples independently across four subpixels before clamping and averaging, as in the original. The sample count is a multiple of four. Resolution is bounded to 640 × 480 and the Wasm memory is fixed at 64 MiB. No image or rendering data leaves the device. The interface optionally loads Google Fonts and falls back to system sans-serif fonts.
+
+Tests instantiate the actual compiled Wasm under Node.js and verify opaque, nonblack pixels, reproducibility with a fixed seed, output changes from sphere colors, and rejected invalid dimensions. No browser verification or development servers were used.
